h using TF-IDF or vector embeddings locally is very promising for quickly finding the top candidates.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Matching Methods**\n\nI'm now diving deeper into optimization. I've realized I have `rapidfuzz`! Why not use it to pre-filter, sending only the top 3 candidates to the LLM? My current approach involves passing the *entire* warehouse catalog to the LLM for every batch, which is insane. I am thinking that `Shortage_A` -> `Fuzzy Top 5 in Warehouse` -> `LLM (Yes/No)` is a good option. I am also aware that Arabic drug names might be missed if spelled completely differently.\n\n\n**Validating Optimizations**\n\nI am now focusing on threading to fix immediate performance issues. Wrapping batch processing in a `ThreadPoolExecutor` seems promising, potentially slashing processing time. I need to test if the rate limits of the `openrouter/free` tier will allow this concurrency. After this, I'll validate the user's vector database idea, converting warehouse items into numerical vectors.\n\n\n\n**Implementing Vector Search**\n\nI'm now implementing the user's vector database suggestion. My plan involves a classic Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) approach to address the N x M complexity. Specifically, I plan to convert both shortages and warehouse items into vectors, followed by using the top-3 closest warehouse vectors for each shortage. These top matches will then be passed to the LLM for a final, quick decision.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"replace_file_content","args":{"AllowMultiple":"false","Description":"\"Implement multithreading to speed up LLM batch matching\"","EndLine":"265","Instruction":"\"Use concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor to run batches in parallel.\"","ReplacementContent":"\"    def _llm_batch_match(\\n        self,\\n        shortage_items: list[dict],\\n        warehouse_items: list[dict],\\n        warehouse_name: str,\\n    ) -> list[dict]:\\n        import concurrent.futures\\n\\n        batch_size = 50\\n        all_results 